To load different sites in one frame, you can use JavaScript to change the `src` attribute of the iframe dynamically. However, keep in mind that due to security restrictions (like the Same-Origin Policy), you may face limitations when trying to interact with content from different domains.

One approach is to use AJAX to load content into a `<div>` instead of using multiple iframes. This method can help manage memory usage better than having multiple iframes. You can create a single iframe and change its `src` attribute to load different sites one after another, but this may not allow you to interact with the content due to cross-origin restrictions. 

For example, you could implement a function like this:

```javascript
function loadSite(url) {
    document.getElementById('myIframe').src = url;
}
```

This function would change the `src` of the iframe to the desired URL. However, if you need to display content from multiple sites without reloading the iframe, consider using AJAX to fetch data and display it in a `<div>` instead, as this avoids the limitations of iframes and can be more efficient [Load multiple external websites into one webpage via re-using ONE IFrame + Ajax](247200).

If you need to communicate between iframes from different domains, you would typically need to use a server-side proxy or ensure both pages set the same `document.domain` if they share a top-level domain [Going beyond the iframe](450960).

To create a custom warning for unsaved changes before leaving a webpage, you can utilize the `window.onbeforeunload` event. However, it's important to note that you cannot fully customize the dialog that appears; you can only append a message to the default dialog.

Here’s a basic implementation:

1. **Track the "dirty" state of your form**: You need a function that checks if the form has unsaved changes. This can be done by comparing the current values of the form elements with their default values.

   ```javascript
   function formIsDirty(form) {
       for (var i = 0; i < form.elements.length; i++) {
           var element = form.elements[i];
           var type = element.type;
           if (type === "checkbox" || type === "radio") {
               if (element.checked !== element.defaultChecked) return true;
           } else if (type === "hidden" || type === "password" || type === "text" || type === "textarea") {
               if (element.value !== element.defaultValue) return true;
           } else if (type === "select-one" || type === "select-multiple") {
               for (var j = 0; j < element.options.length; j++) {
                   if (element.options[j].selected !== element.options[j].defaultSelected) return true;
               }
           }
       }
       return false;
   }
   ```

2. **Set up the `onbeforeunload` event**: You can then set the `onbeforeunload` event to trigger when the form is dirty.

   ```javascript
   window.onbeforeunload = function(e) {
       e = e || window.event;
       if (formIsDirty(document.forms["yourFormName"])) {
           var message = "You have unsaved changes!";
           // For IE and Firefox
           if (e) {
               e.returnValue = message;
           }
           // For Safari
           return message;
       }
   };
   ```

This code will prompt the user with a warning if they attempt to leave the page with unsaved changes. Remember that the exact text of the dialog cannot be fully customized, as the browser will display its own default message along with your custom message [Client/JS Framework for "Unsaved Data" Protection?](140460) [How can I override the OnBeforeUnload dialog and replace it with my own?](276660). 

Make sure to replace `"yourFormName"` with the actual name of your form.

