To stop fetching external definitions, such as DTDs, while processing an XML document in Perl, you can use the `XML::Parser` module with the `NoLWP` option set to `1`. This prevents the parser from using LWP to resolve external entities. You can create a custom parser and pass it to `XML::XPath` like this:

```perl
use XML::XPath;
use XML::Parser;

my $p = XML::Parser->new(NoLWP => 1);
my $xp = XML::XPath->new(parser => $p, filename => "a.xhtml");
```

Keep in mind that by doing this, you will lose all entities except for numerical ones and the default ones (like `>`, `<`, `&`, `'`, and `"`). If you are looking for a more powerful and actively maintained alternative, consider using `XML::LibXML`, which has a similar interface and is generally faster and more capable than `XML::XPath` [How can I prevent XML::XPath from fetching a DTD while processing an XML file?](303510).

To create a query string from a list of field names in Java, you can use a method similar to the one described in the context. You can iterate over a list of field names, encode them, and concatenate them into a query string format. Here's a simple implementation based on the provided context:

